      "topic_path": "Chapter 3: Hamlet King Prince/Pico First",
      "content": "If Adam was the first gardener, he was also the first to come to\ndust-to what Hamlet will call the \"quintessence of dust,\" in a speech\nthat closely parallels one of the most famous of all Renaissance humanist\ndocuments, Pico della Mirandola 's Oration on the Dignity of Man But\nwhere Pico celebrated the mutability of mankind, Hamlet regards it with\nsuspicion, even with dread Here is Pico:\n[U]pon man, at the moment of his creation, God bestowed seeds\npregnant with all possibilities, the germs of every form of life Whichever of these a man shall cultivate, the same will mature and\nbear fruit in him If vegetative, he will become a plant; if sensual, he\nwill become brutish; if rational, he will reveal himself a heavenly\nbeing; if intellectual, he will be an angel and the son of God",
